// cinzel-demo — Cinzel, an all-capitals titling serif in the Trajan tradition, bundled for display lines.
// It has no lowercase and no italic; the cuts sit on the weight axis, selected as the free-axis tags after
// the size: \font cinzel 24 regular, medium, semibold, bold, extrabold, black.

\use{document}

\title{The \texttt{cinzel} Titling Face}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.20.2}
\maketitle

\section{The weights}

Cinzel is an inscriptional capital in the manner of the Roman monumental letter, cut in six weights and no
italic. Because it has no lowercase, it is a face for a line of display — a title, a chapter opening, a cover
— rather than for running text.

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 regular Regular}

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 medium Medium}

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 semibold Semibold}

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 bold Bold}

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 extrabold Extrabold}

\noindent{\font cinzel 22 black Black}

\section{At display size}

\noindent{\font cinzel 40 bold In the beginning}

\vskip 6pt

\noindent{\font cinzel 28 regular Chapter the First}

\vfill\eject
